------------------------------------------------------------------------
Version 1.3 of rdb
------------------------------------------------------------------------

rdb - edit the RDB (Rigid Disk Block) area of an AmigaDOS disk device


This program functions in place of HDToolBox for controllers which
support the RDB area, but refuse to work with HDToolBox.  My IVS
TrumpCard is one such beast.  The rdb utility allows you to tweak most
RDB parameters (including those HDToolBox won't let you touch), so if
you don't know what you are doing, I don't recommend you use this program.

Usage:
    rdb <disk.device> <unit> [<option> | <keyword>=<value> ...]

Where 
    disk.device is the physical disk device driver

    unit is the physical disk unit on that driver

    option can be any of:
        -check = report problems in the RDB
        -fix = fix problems in the RDB, asking before writing
        -edit = interactive RDB and partition editor (like fdisk)
        -newrdb = create a new Rigid Disk Block on a disk which has none
        -rigid = edit fields in the Rigid Disk Block
        -part NAME = modify partition table parameters of NAME
        -newpart NAME = add new partition NAME to the RDB
        -promote NAME = make partition NAME first to be mounted
        -extract DOSTYPE = extract filesystem to a file 
        -avail = report next available block in RDB
        -seglist = show seglist addresses for filesystems
        -help = give more help
        
    keyword can be any of:
        Fields in the RDB disk header
            HostID BlockBytes RigidFlags BadBlockList PartitionList
            FileSysHeaderList DriveInit Cylinders Sectors Heads
            Interleave Park WritePreComp ReducedWrite StepRate
            RDBBlocksLo RDBBlocksHi LoCylinder HiCylinder CylBlocks
            AutoParkSeconds DiskVendor DiskProduct DiskRevision
            ControllerVendor ControllerProduct ControllerRev
        Fields in the Partition header
            Next Flags DevFlags DriveName Surfaces BlocksPerTrack
            Reserved PreAlloc Interleave LowCyl HighCyl NumBuffers
            BufMemType MaxTransfer Mask BootPri DosType

Lackage:
    The program is able to dump filesystem handlers from the
    rdb area to a regular DOS file.  It should also allow
    transfers in the opposite direction.  ie: Installation of
    a new filesystem handler in the rdb area.  It doesn't allow
    this today.

Coolness:
    rdb is able to repair damaged RDB areas (well, it can fix
    the checksum on RDB blocks for you).

    Parameters to rdb may appear in any order and are case-
    insensitive.  Also, the "=" is optional for settings. Rdb
    will ask before making any changes to your disk structure.


Example:
    You want to change the boot priority of DH0: on your machine
    using scsi.device, unit 6
        rdb scsi.device 6 -part DH0 bootpri=10

Creating an RDB:
    -newrdb writes a new RDB to block 0 of a disk which does not
    already have one.  The geometry is taken from the device driver
    (TD_GETGEOMETRY); the RDB area is the first cylinders of the disk
    (at least 2048 blocks), and the rest of the disk is available for
    partitions.  RDB keywords override any field, for example:
        rdb a4091.device 3 -newrdb Heads=16 Sectors=63 DiskVendor=CDH
    -newpart may be given at the same time to add the first partition:
        rdb a4091.device 3 -newrdb -newpart DH9 DosType=0x444f5303

    -newpart takes Surfaces and BlocksPerTrack from the RDB geometry,
    and LowCyl and HighCyl from the RDB partitionable area, unless they
    are given.  It refuses a partition which overlaps the RDB area,
    another partition, or the end of the disk.
    rdb warns if the first blocks of the disk are not empty, and always
    asks before writing.  Only 512-byte sectors are supported.

Checking and fixing an RDB:
    -check reads every block of the RDB (bad block list, partitions,
    filesystems and their code, and DriveInit code) and reports
    errors and warnings.  It checks block IDs and checksums, broken
    or looping lists, blocks used twice, the RDB geometry, and each
    partition: name (missing or duplicate), cylinder range (overlaps
    the RDB area, another partition, or runs past the end of the disk),
    boot blocks, buffers, and a DosType with no filesystem.  The exit
    status is 1 if errors were found.
        rdb scsi.device 0 -check

    -fix also repairs what can be repaired safely: checksums, invalid
    SummedLongs in RDB/partition/filesystem blocks, broken or looping
    lists (the list is ended at the last good block), HiCylinder,
    HighRDSKBlock, and missing or duplicate partition names.  It shows
    the blocks it will write and asks first, then checks the RDB again.
    Overlapping partitions, damaged filesystem code, and blocks used
    by two lists are reported but not changed.

Editing interactively:
    -edit shows the partition table and accepts these commands:
        p   print the partition table
        n   add a new partition (name, first and last cylinder or
            +size{K,M,G}, DosType)
        d   delete a partition
        e   edit a partition's fields (name, DosType, buffers, bootable,
            boot priority, automount, cylinders, MaxTransfer, Mask, ...)
        r   edit RDB fields
        l   list the filesystems in the RDB (DosType, version, size,
            blocks, stack, and the partitions which use each one)
        a   add a filesystem from a file, such as L:FastFileSystem.
            The version is read from the file's $VER string; the
            DosType, version, and stack size may be given.
        x   remove a filesystem (asks first if a partition needs it)
        g   create a new RDB (shown only when the disk has none)
        c   check for problems
        f   fix problems
        w   write the changes to disk and quit
        q   quit without saving
    Nothing is written until w, which lists the blocks and asks first.
        rdb a4091.device 3 -edit

